557 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 84 0 R
/T (Theory: Socket options in Java allow developers to fine-tune the behavior of the underlying network protocols \(like TCP\) to optimize performance, handle edge cases, or adjust latency. SO_KEEPALIVE ensures inactive connections are checked periodically. SO_LINGER specifies how long to wait before forcefully closing a socket if un-sent data remains. SO_SNDBUF and SO_RCVBUF set hints for the size of the underlying network I/O buffers. Finally, TCP_NODELAY disables Nagle's algorithm, forcing packets to be sent immediately rather than waiting to fill a buffer, which is crucial for time-sensitive or low-latency applications.)
/E (Theory: Socket options in Java allow developers to fine-tune the behavior of the underlying network protocols \(like TCP\) to optimize performance, handle edge cases, or adjust latency. SO_KEEPALIVE ensures inactive connections are checked periodically. SO_LINGER specifies how long to wait before forcefully closing a socket if un-sent data remains. SO_SNDBUF and SO_RCVBUF set hints for the size of the underlying network I/O buffers. Finally, TCP_NODELAY disables Nagle's algorithm, forcing packets to be sent immediately rather than waiting to fill a buffer, which is crucial for time-sensitive or low-latency applications.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
